"text":"rendering using stream","fontSize":16,"fontFamily":5,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"originalText":"rendering using stream","autoResize":true,"lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"KSsaoVsLYpXlx1YPfyPBu","type":"text","x":-36805.22219052687,"y":1785.483024487301,"width":778.911376953125,"height":100,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"#ffffff","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":2,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"index":"bql","roundness":null,"seed":1138007075,"version":88,"versionNonce":760224099,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1768795198536,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"React SSR renders HTML on the server so users see content immediately, then hydrates it on the\nclient for interactivity. In React 18, streaming with Suspense lets you send the shell first and\nprogressively reveal slow sections, improving perceived performance and resilience. It’s ideal for SEO\nand mixed-latency pages, but requires careful caching, error boundaries, and metrics to ensure real-\nworld gains.","fontSize":16,"fontFamily":5,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"originalText":"React SSR renders HTML on the server so users see content immediately, then hydrates it on the client for interactivity. In React 18, streaming with Suspense lets you send the shell first and progressively reveal slow sections, improving perceived performance and resilience. It’s ideal for SEO and mixed-latency pages, but requires careful caching, error boundaries, and metrics to ensure real-world gains.","autoResize":false,"lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"lvHz-ef_sJEPjgSTuB1LR","type":"text","x":-35309.78041196831,"y":1067.6277933333622,"width":791.8873291015625,"height":60,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"#ffffff","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":2,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"index":"bqm","roundness":null,"seed":332603331,"version":191,"versionNonce":576905805,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1768795198536,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"Incremental Server Rendering is when the server streams HTML to the browser in chunks as parts of\nthe page become ready, instead of waiting for the entire page. Users see the shell and fast sections\nimmediately, and slower sections reveal progressively, then hydrate for interactivity.","fontSize":16,"fontFamily":5,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"originalText":"Incremental Server Rendering is when the server streams HTML to the browser in chunks as parts of the page become ready, instead of waiting for the entire page. Users see the shell and fast sections immediately, and slower sections reveal progressively, then hydrate for interactivity.","autoResize":false,"lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"cNSnkb8I_v__7zUfjNsu9","type":"text","x":-35311.86836362847,"y":1157.6277933333622,"width":796.063232421875,"height":80,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"#ffffff","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":2,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"index":"bqn","roundness":null,"seed":1417686883,"version":191,"versionNonce":1399649539,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1768795198536,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"It improves perceived performance and resilience—critical content renders early, while slow or optional\ndata loads later. It’s great for dashboards or product pages with mixed-latency data. I typically use\nReact 18 Suspense or Next.js App Router to define boundaries and stream them, with placeholders or\nskeletons to avoid layout shifts.","fontSize":16,"fontFamily":5,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"originalText":"It improves perceived performance and resilience—critical content renders early, while slow or optional data loads later. It’s great for dashboards or product pages with mixed-latency data. I typically use React 18 Suspense or Next.js App Router to define boundaries and stream them, with placeholders or skeletons to avoid layout shifts.","autoResize":false,"lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"af0ZD8I-Fk295ttCxGPyu","type":"text","x":-35304.28440977105,"y":1267.6277933333622,"width":780.8953247070312,"height":100,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"#ffffff","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":2,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"index":"bqo","roundness":null,"seed":136989443,"version":191,"versionNonce":499847341,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":[],"updated":1768795198536,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"Trade-offs include more complex caching and error handling, and you must instrument metrics like\nTTFB and LCP to confirm real-world gains. It’s different from Incremental Static Regeneration,\nwhich regenerates static pages in the background but doesn’t stream. In practice, I cache the shell\nat the edge, stream slow panels, and handle failures with error boundaries so the page never goes\nblank.","fontSize":16,"fontFamily":5,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","containerId":null,"originalText":"Trade-offs include more complex caching and error handling, and you must instrument metrics like TTFB and LCP to confirm real-world gains. It’s different from Incremental Static Regeneration, which regenerates static pages in the background but doesn’t stream. In practice, I cache the shell at the edge, stream slow panels, and handle failures with error boundaries so the page never goes blank.","autoResize":false,"lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"WVYrgLXoh_rP-Nm6DD9hb","type":"text","x":-28653.473126460915,"y":-442.11223752517253,"width":98.81385443357158,"height":43.46890126115634,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"#ffffff","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":2,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"index":"bqp","roundness":null,"seed":1666183843,"version":492,"versionNo